        q: "How would you structure a Node.js and Express.js backend project?",
        a_en: "The standard layered structure separates routes, which are thin and just wire URLs to controllers; controllers, which handle req/res parsing and shaping but hold no business logic; services, which contain the actual business rules and validations; repositories, which are pure database access; models for schemas; and separate folders for middlewares, config, utils and validators. Keeping app.js, the Express setup, separate from server.js, which actually starts the HTTP listener, matters for testability, since tests can import the app without binding a real port. This separation makes code testable because services don't know anything about HTTP, maintainable because logic lives in one predictable place, and scalable for a team since different people can work on different layers without stepping on each other.",
        a_hi: "Standard layered structure me routes thin hote hain, sirf URL ko controller se wire karte hain; controllers req/res parse-shape karte hain lekin unme business logic nahi hoti; services me actual business rules aur validations hote hain; repositories sirf database access karte hain; models me schemas hote hain; aur middlewares, config, utils, validators ke alag folders hote hain. app.js (Express setup) ko server.js (jo actually HTTP listener start karta hai) se alag rakhna testability ke liye zaroori hai, kyunki tests app ko import kar sakte hain bina real port bind kiye. Yeh separation code ko testable banata hai kyunki services ko HTTP ka pata hi nahi hota, maintainable banata hai kyunki logic ek predictable jagah hoti hai, aur team ke liye scalable banata hai kyunki alag log alag layers pe kaam kar sakte hain bina ek doosre ko touch kiye.",
