        q: "What's the difference between blocking and non-blocking code in Node.js, with an example?",
        a_en: "Blocking code halts further JavaScript execution until the current operation completes — fs.readFileSync('file.txt') freezes the entire single thread, unable to serve any other request, until that file finishes reading. Non-blocking code, like fs.readFile('file.txt', callback), initiates the operation and immediately returns control, letting the thread go on to handle other requests while libuv works on the file read in the background, invoking the callback only once that background work is actually done.",
        a_hi: "Blocking code aage ki JavaScript execution ko tab tak rok deta hai jab tak current operation complete na ho jaaye — fs.readFileSync('file.txt') poore single thread ko freeze kar deta hai, koi bhi doosri request serve nahi kar paata, jab tak file poori padh na li jaaye. Non-blocking code, jaise fs.readFile('file.txt', callback), operation shuru karta hai aur turant control wapas kar deta hai, thread ko baaki requests handle karne deta hai jabki libuv background me file read pe kaam karta hai, aur woh background kaam actually poora hone pe hi callback invoke karta hai.",
