        q: "How do you manage environment variables and secrets securely in production?",
        a_en: "Never commit secrets to git — .env should always be listed in .gitignore, with only a .env.example template checked in to document which variables are expected. In production, secrets are typically injected by the hosting platform directly, such as through Vercel, AWS, or Kubernetes secrets, or managed through a dedicated secrets manager like AWS Secrets Manager or HashiCorp Vault, ideally rotated periodically rather than left static indefinitely, with access to them restricted to only the specific services that genuinely need each one.",
