        q: "How would you design communication between multiple microservices?",
        a_en: "Synchronous communication, typically REST or gRPC calls between services, fits requests that genuinely need an immediate response, such as an order service needing to confirm current inventory before proceeding. Asynchronous communication through a message queue or event bus like Kafka or RabbitMQ fits events that other services care about but don't need to block on, such as an order.placed event that a notification service and an analytics service both separately consume. Real-world systems typically combine both, alongside patterns like circuit breakers to stop a failing downstream service from cascading its failure into every service that calls it, and a service registry or API gateway so consuming services don't need to hardcode each other's network locations.",
        a_hi: "Synchronous communication (typically REST ya gRPC calls services ke beech) un requests ke liye fit hai jinhe genuinely turant response chahiye, jaise ek order service ko aage badhne se pehle current inventory confirm karni ho. Asynchronous communication ek message queue ya event bus (Kafka, RabbitMQ) ke through un events ke liye fit hai jinme baaki services interested hain lekin unpe block hone ki zaroorat nahi, jaise ek order.placed event jise ek notification service aur ek analytics service dono alag-alag consume karein. Real-world systems typically dono ko combine karte hain, saath me circuit breakers jaise patterns bhi (taaki ek failing downstream service apni failure ko har use call karne wali service tak cascade na kar de), aur ek service registry ya API gateway (taaki consuming services ko ek doosre ki network locations hardcode na karni padein).",
