        q: "What's the biggest challenge you'd expect when running Node.js at real scale, and how would you approach it?",
        a_en: "Node's single-threaded event loop is the recurring theme behind most scaling pain: any accidentally CPU-heavy synchronous code — a poorly written data transformation, a regex that backtracks catastrophically — blocks the entire process and every concurrent request behind it, not just the one that triggered it. The approach is offloading genuinely CPU-heavy work to worker_threads or a separate background job queue, clustering across CPU cores for pure I/O-bound throughput, aggressive caching with Redis to keep the database from becoming the bottleneck instead, and continuous APM monitoring to actually catch a creeping regression before it becomes a full production incident rather than after.",
        a_hi: "Node ka single-threaded event loop zyaadatar scaling pain ke peeche ka recurring theme hai: koi bhi accidentally CPU-heavy synchronous code — badly likha hua data transformation, ek catastrophically backtrack karne wala regex — poore process ko block kar deta hai, aur uske peeche wali har concurrent request ko bhi, sirf trigger karne wali request ko nahi. Approach yeh hai: genuinely CPU-heavy kaam ko worker_threads ya ek separate background job queue me offload karo, pure I/O-bound throughput ke liye CPU cores ke aar-paar cluster karo, database ko bottleneck banne se rokne ke liye Redis se aggressive caching karo, aur continuous APM monitoring rakho taaki ek creeping regression ko full production incident banne se pehle hi actually pakad liya jaaye, baad me nahi.",
